{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgg2Ti395AEJuxoLQzxit","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Results:** Dq ≈ 2660 cm⁻¹ (10Dq = 26 600 cm⁻¹), B ≈ 529 cm⁻¹, C ≈ 2770 cm⁻¹. I solved these by hand with the standard d³ expressions and did not use the tools.\n\n**Dq.** For d³ octahedral, the ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₂ transition equals 10Dq. So 10Dq = 26 600 cm⁻¹ and Dq = 2660 cm⁻¹.\n\n**B.** The ⁴T₁(F) level comes from the 2×2 secular problem that mixes ⁴T₁(F) and ⁴T₁(P). Its lower root is\n\nν₂ = 7.5B + 1.5Δ − ½√(225B² − 18BΔ + Δ²), with Δ = 10Dq = 26 600 cm⁻¹.\n\nSetting ν₂ = 32 500 cm⁻¹:\n\n- 7.5B + 39 900 − 32 500 = ½√(…), so 15B + 14 800 = √(225B² − 478 800B + 707.56×10⁶).\n- Squaring gives 225B² + 444 000B + 219.04×10⁶ = 225B² − 478 800B + 707.56×10⁶.\n- The B² terms cancel, so 922 800B = 488.52×10⁶ and B ≈ 529 cm⁻¹.\n\n**C.** I used the approximate expression for the spin-flip ²E level, E(²E) ≈ 9B + 3C − 24B²/Δ. This is an approximation, so C carries more uncertainty than Dq and B.\n\n- 12 820 = 9(529.4) + 3C − 24(529.4)²/26 600.\n- 12 820 = 4765 + 3C − 253, so 3C ≈ 8308 and C ≈ 2770 cm⁻¹.\n\nThis gives C/B ≈ 5.2, which is reasonable for a strongly covalent cyanide complex.\n\n{\"Dq\": 2660, \"B\": 529, \"C\": 2770, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Dq from ν1=10Dq, B from the 4T1 secular equation using ν2, C from the approximate 2E energy.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7996,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7996,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed2e61e0-f9b2-4427-9b00-71906458d82f","uuid":"87a1e4da-3e01-4fc1-b40a-63d8b7b5178c","timestamp":"2026-11-03T12:52:24.